{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Better is open rebuke than hidden love (Proverbs 27:5).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, made known to us in his book great principles concerning the trait of reproof, and taught the people knowledge that life is bound to reproof, and death is bound to one who hates reproof. That life is bound to reproof is what is written (Proverbs 27:6): ״ודרך חיים תוכחות מוסר״, and it is written (ibid. 10): ״אורח לחיים שומר מוסר״, and it is written (ibid. 15): ״מרפא לשון עץ חיים״ — he says that the healings of the tongue, which is reproof, are the tree of life. And that death is bound to one who hates reproof is what is written (ibid. 10): ״מוסר רע לעוזב אורח שונא תוכחת ימות״ — he says that one who leaves the path, that is, one who transgresses the words of Torah occasionally but does not hate reproof, will be punished with 'מוסר רע' — HaShem, may His Name be exalted, will chastise him, so that he may turn back from his evil way; but one who hates reproof, chastisement will not suffice for him, for he has no remedy, and therefore he will be punished with death. Love of reproof is a sign and proof of goodness of character traits, and hatred of reproof is a great testimony to the evil of one's nature and to the deficiency of character traits; and concerning this the verse says (Proverbs 9): ״אל תוכח לץ פן ישנאך הוכח לחכם ויאהבך״. One who rebukes his fellow owes it to him to reveal to him the hidden truths and should not flatter him, but rather vindicate him in what is fitting and condemn him in what is fitting — for the righteous and the wicked are two opposites, and one who escapes the way of the trait of the wicked is not called righteous until he draws near to the level of the righteous, because the trait of the wicked is at one extreme and the trait of the righteous at the other extreme, and this one who escapes the trait of the wicked is in the middle. For this reason the one who rebukes should reveal to the one rebuked the whole truth concerning his affairs and his deeds, for this is the essential reproof. And therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, said here (Proverbs 27): ״טובה תוכחת מגולה מאהבה מסותרת״ — better is one who rebukes his fellow and speaks harshly to him and informs him of the truth to his face and shows him hatred in the words of his mouth, than one who has hidden love, who loves his fellow in the chambers of his heart but does not rebuke him for what he sees in him. And some interpreted ״טובה תוכחת מגולה״ as the verse joining its beginning to its end — that is, better is open rebuke when it comes from hidden love, meaning that the one who rebukes rebukes him out of love. But the Kimhi, of blessed memory, interpreted in the name of his father, of blessed memory: ״טובה תוכחת מגולה״ — reproof, when it is open, is good, but when it is from hidden love — meaning that reproof should be in private, not in public, so as not to shame him. And they said in the Midrash of Eileh HaDevarim Rabbah: ״טובה תוכחת מגולה״ — these are the reproofs of Moses; ״מאהבה מסותרת״ — this is Balaam the wicked, who would bless Israel but had one thing in his heart and another in his mouth; he came to say, ״מה טובו אהליך מואב משכנותיך בני עמון״, and the Holy One, blessed be He, bent his mouth and said, ״מה טובו אהליך יעקב משכנותך ישראל״ — but the reproofs of Moses were open. And it is possible to interpret ״טובה תוכחת מגולה״ — these are the reproofs that are in the Torah of the Priests and in the Mishneh Torah, in which not even one is from Moses himself but all are from the mouth of the Almighty — this is what it says, ״מאהבה מסותרת״, and that is the hidden Shekhinah, which is called 'love,' in the manner of what is written (ibid. 15): ״טוב ארוחת ירק ואהבה שם״, and its meaning is the Shekhinah, in the manner of (Song 2): ״אם תעירו ואם תעוררו את האהבה״, (ibid. 8): ״אם יתן איש את כל הון ביתו באהבה״, and likewise in the prayer 'to give thanks to You and to unify You in love.' And therefore it says, ״טובה תוכחת מגולה מאהבה״ — coming from love, that is, from the Shekhinah. And what is its goodness? That even though all the reproofs have passed over Israel and have been fulfilled upon them — these in the First Temple and these in the Second Temple — they have all passed and have come for their good, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 8): ״למען ענותך ולמען נסותך להיטבך באחריתך״, and all the reproofs perish but Israel endure; and this is what the prophet said (Malachi 3): ״אני ה' לא שניתי ואתם בני יעקב לא כליתם״, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: the nations of the world fall with one fall and have no rising, as it is said (Psalms 36): ״שם נפלו פועלי און דחו ולא יכלו קום״; but Israel fall many falls and have a rising, as it is said (Micah 7): ״אל תשמחי אויבתי לי כי נפלתי קמתי״. And this is what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted (Deuteronomy 32): ״חצי אכלה בם״ — half are consumed, but they are not consumed. To what may the matter be compared? To a warrior who set up a beam and kept shooting arrows at it — the arrows perish but the beam stands. So too Israel fall many falls and suffer many afflictions; the afflictions perish, and they stand in their place. And this is what Jeremiah, peace be upon him, said (Lamentations 3): ״דרך קשתו ויציבני כמטרא לחץ״. <b>And this is</b> what Moses said in his reproof." "<b>You are standing today, all of you, before YHWH your Elohim — your heads, your tribes, your elders, and your officers, every man of Israel.</b> All Israel were now gathered here into the second covenant, just as they were gathered into the first covenant at the standing at Mount Sinai; and because the first covenant that was at Sinai was annulled when they said (Exodus 32): ״אלה אלהיך ישראל״, therefore he returned here, in the land of Moab, to make another covenant with them, and they accepted it upon themselves with imprecation and oath. And from this Moses says to them: 'You are standing today, all of you, before YHWH your Elohim' — that is, this assembly in which you are in this covenant, you are all standing before HaShem, may His Name be exalted, just as your fathers were at Mount Sinai in the first covenant; this means that you shall enter the covenant of HaShem and accept it upon yourselves with imprecation and oath, which was not done in the first. And it is possible to say that two promises are included in this verse: one, that Israel shall endure and never be annulled, but shall be a latter generation and stand forever; and the second, that they shall merit the life of the World to Come through the fulfillment of the Torah. And he needed to promise thus because Israel deserved to be destroyed on account of the severity of the reproofs that he mentioned above, which had passed over them; and therefore he said that they shall be 'standing today' in this world, in the language of (Psalms 119): ״לעולם ה' דברך נצב בשמים״, and they shall not perish on account of the harsh reproofs, but those reproofs shall be for them an expiation of sins through which they shall merit the life of the World to Come — and this is what it says, 'before YHWH your Elohim,' in the language of (Deuteronomy 6): ״וצדקה תהיה לנו לפני ה' אלהינו״, which is a promise concerning the reward of the World to Come. And this is the language of 'all of you (כלכם),' in the language of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, who said: all Israel have a portion in the World to Come." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'You are standing today' — just as this day, its beginning is dark and afterward it grows light, so your darkness is destined to shine, as it is said (Isaiah 60): ״והיה לך ה' לאור עולם ואלהיך לתפארתך״." "<b>Your heads, your tribes.</b> That is, your heads, who are the heads of your tribes. And he mentioned the dignitaries first, then the men — 'every man of Israel' — then the little ones among the males, then the women, then the converts." "<b>Every man of Israel.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory deduced from here that all Israel are held accountable for the sin of one man, and the proof is Achan, who trespassed in the devoted things, and the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Joshua (Joshua 7): ״חטא ישראל״ — 'Israel has sinned' — and all Israel were held accountable for his sin. And this is the sense of כל איש ישראל — that is, all Israel are held accountable for the sin of one man (איש אחד). And this is what is written (Leviticus 26): ״וכשלו איש באחיו״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted it: in the sin of his brother — to teach you that all Israel are guarantors for one another. And if in punishment we find that the many are held accountable for the sin of the individual, how much more so that the many are saved through the merit of the individual. And so Eliphaz said to Job (Job 22): ״ימלט אי נקי״, that is, one innocent man saves the whole island, and if the truth is with you that you are innocent and righteous, the whole city would be saved through the hollow of your hand. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: for the sake of one righteous man the world stands, as it says (Proverbs 10): ״וצדיק יסוד עולם״ — for the measure of good exceeds the measure of punishment."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:22.486487321Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}